# ── gisik-nokma configuration contract ─────────────────────────────────────
# Every variable the server and web read. scripts/check-env-example.mjs
# (pnpm check:env, run in CI) fails if code reads a variable this file
# does not document. Copy to .env and adjust; never commit .env.
#
# Convention: each section names the plan that READS its variables, and a
# variable whose plan has not landed is documented ahead of its reader, not
# live — setting it today changes nothing, because no shipped code looks at
# it. This product does not assert what it has not checked, and an operator
# must not be told to configure something inert without being told so.
#
# LIVE TODAY (everything else in this file is documented ahead of its reader):
#   DATABASE_URL, PORT, HOST, LOG_LEVEL, BETTER_AUTH_URL, BETTER_AUTH_SECRET,
#   WEB_ORIGIN, SNAPSHOT_INTERVAL_MS, SNAPSHOT_MIN_EVENTS
#     — exactly apps/server/src/config.ts's zod schema. BETTER_AUTH_* arrived
#       with Plan 4 Task 2, WEB_ORIGIN with Plan 4 Task 14 (it is the host of
#       every invitation link the server mints, so it stopped being inert the
#       moment invites shipped); the first four are Plan 1's. A missing or invalid
#       one stops the server at loadConfig, naming the variable.
#   GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID, GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET
#     — read directly by createAuth (apps/server/src/auth/auth.ts), not through
#       config.ts. Empty is legal: it leaves the provider registered but
#       unusable rather than failing start.
#   OBJECT_STORE, OBJECT_STORE_DIR, OBJECT_STORE_S3_REGION, OBJECT_STORE_S3_BUCKET,
#   OBJECT_STORE_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID, OBJECT_STORE_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY,
#   OBJECT_STORE_S3_ENDPOINT, OBJECT_STORE_S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE
#     — read by apps/server/src/attachments/store-config.ts's
#       createObjectStoreFromEnv (Plan 7 Task 8), not through config.ts.
#       NOTE: the "Object storage" section below (S3_ENDPOINT, S3_REGION,
#       S3_BUCKET, S3_ACCESS_KEY, S3_SECRET_KEY, S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE) predates
#       this task and names none of these; no shipped code reads that block
#       either. Left as-is rather than reconciled here — that block belongs
#       to whichever plan wrote it, not to this task.
#   TEST_DATABASE_ADMIN_URL — read by the test-database helper.
#
# `pnpm check:env` proves every variable code READS is documented here. It does
# NOT check that the documented VALUES are valid — apps/server/test/config.test.ts
# does that, by feeding this file's own values to loadConfig.

# Core (Plan 1)
DATABASE_URL=postgres://gisik:gisik@localhost:54329/gisik_nokma
PORT=3000
HOST=127.0.0.1                       # 0.0.0.0 in containers
LOG_LEVEL=info                       # fatal|error|warn|info|debug|trace

# Snapshot writer (Plan 10 Task 5). Snapshots bound per-entity replay cost:
# every SNAPSHOT_INTERVAL_MS the server folds each ticket that has run
# SNAPSHOT_MIN_EVENTS past its latest snapshot and stores the result. Neither
# is a correctness knob — a server that never writes one answers with exactly
# the same state, only slower. Both must be positive integers; the server
# refuses to start otherwise, and a zero on either is a real hazard rather
# than a silly value (a 0 interval runs the pass on every event-loop tick).
SNAPSHOT_INTERVAL_MS=300000          # five minutes
SNAPSHOT_MIN_EVENTS=20               # events past the last snapshot before a new one

# Time (owner decision 2026-08-20: time is Asia/Kolkata). TZ sets the Node
# process's own zone, so logs and any runtime Date formatting agree with the
# product's screens. Read by the runtime, not by application code — so the
# check-env gate never sees it; it is documented here because an operator
# needs to know it exists. The DATABASE's zone is set separately, on the
# database itself (docker-compose sets it with `-c timezone=Asia/Kolkata`
# locally; deploy/do/README.md documents the managed-Postgres step, and
# `pnpm check:db-tz` verifies it — BOTH land with Plan 11 and do not exist
# yet, so neither is a command you can run today).
TZ=Asia/Kolkata

# Managed-Postgres TLS (production; leave empty for local)
DATABASE_CA_CERT=

# Auth (Plan 4 / better-auth) — LIVE. Both required; no defaults.
# BETTER_AUTH_SECRET signs every session cookie and bearer token and must be at
# least 32 characters: that is better-auth's own floor, below which it warns and
# starts anyway, so config.ts refuses instead. The value below is a placeholder
# of legal length and is NOT a secret — generate a real one with
# `openssl rand -base64 32` and rotate per runbook. The previous value here was
# `change-me`, nine characters, which made this file's own "copy to .env and
# adjust" instruction end in ConfigError.
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=replace-me-with-openssl-rand-base64-32
BETTER_AUTH_URL=http://localhost:3000
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=                    # Google OAuth (spec §6); empty disables
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Web origin + proxy trust. WEB_ORIGIN is LIVE (Plan 4 Task 14 reads it through
# config.ts): it is the host of every invitation link, so a wrong value mints
# links that go somewhere else. TRUST_PROXY is still Plan 11's and still inert.
WEB_ORIGIN=http://localhost:3001
TRUST_PROXY=false                    # true behind App Platform's router

# Rate limits (Plan 11) — production defaults 10 and 120; dev/CI run looser
RATE_LIMIT_AUTH_MAX=10
RATE_LIMIT_EVENTS_MAX=120
RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SEC=60

# Magic-link email (Plan 11) — empty SMTP_URL falls back to the log sender
SMTP_URL=smtp://localhost:1025
MAGIC_LINK_FROM=sign-in@gisik-nokma.local

# Error alerting (Plan 11) — any Slack-compatible webhook; empty = noop
ALERT_WEBHOOK_URL=

# Object storage (Plan 7 attachments + Plan 11 backups; S3 protocol —
# MinIO locally, DO Spaces or S3 in production)
S3_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:9000
S3_REGION=us-east-1
S3_BUCKET=gisik-attachments
S3_ACCESS_KEY=gisik
S3_SECRET_KEY=gisik-dev-secret
S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE=true             # true for MinIO; false for Spaces/S3

# Attachment object store (Plan 7 Task 8) — LIVE. OBJECT_STORE selects the
# implementation createObjectStoreFromEnv builds: 'local' (default; the dev/
# test disk store) writes under OBJECT_STORE_DIR; 's3' talks the S3 protocol
# and requires all four OBJECT_STORE_S3_* credentials below, or
# createObjectStoreFromEnv refuses with every missing one named in a single
# ConfigError. Any other OBJECT_STORE value is refused by name, too.
# OBJECT_STORE_S3_ENDPOINT is optional (omit it against real AWS S3, where
# the SDK derives the endpoint from the region); path-style addressing
# defaults true because that is what every S3-compatible endpoint (MinIO,
# R2) needs, not because it is AWS's own default.
#
# This is a SEPARATE variable set from the "Object storage" block just
# above: that block predates this task, names none of these variables, and
# no shipped code reads it either — left alone rather than reconciled here
# (see the file header's NOTE). Whether the two should merge is still open.
#
# SETTLED by Plan 11 Task 11 (2026-09-06): the BACKUP daemon SHARES these
# five credentials and takes only its BUCKET from its own variable
# (BACKUP_S3_BUCKET, below), so a backup can never land in the attachment
# bucket. apps/server/src/ops/object-sink.ts is stricter than
# createObjectStoreFromEnv about two of them, deliberately: it REFUSES to
# boot without OBJECT_STORE_S3_ENDPOINT and OBJECT_STORE_S3_REGION, naming
# them. A backup destination has no local-disk fallback to degrade to, and
# an unset endpoint resolves to Amazon's while an unset region used to
# default to 'us-east-1' — a wrong-cloud upload that dev never sees,
# because the MinIO values below supply both. Attachments keep the endpoint
# optional (real AWS S3 derives it from the region); backups do not.
OBJECT_STORE=local
OBJECT_STORE_DIR=var/attachments
OBJECT_STORE_S3_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:9000
OBJECT_STORE_S3_REGION=us-east-1
OBJECT_STORE_S3_BUCKET=gisik-attachments
OBJECT_STORE_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=gisik
OBJECT_STORE_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=gisik-dev-secret
OBJECT_STORE_S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE=true             # true for MinIO; false for Spaces/S3

# Backups (Plan 11)
BACKUP_S3_BUCKET=gisik-backups
BACKUP_TIME_UTC=21:00                # nightly pg_dump; 21:00 UTC = 02:30 IST
LEDGER_EXPORT_TIME_UTC=22:00         # weekly ledger export
LEDGER_EXPORT_DOW_UTC=0              # 0 = Sunday

# Seeds (Plan 11) — people are looked up, never invented
SEED_OWNER_EMAIL=
SEED_ACCOUNTANT_EMAIL=

# Web client (Plan 6 Task 14)
#
# Two variables, and the two the plan text named are gone because the shipped
# shell reads neither. NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL had no reader: apps/web mounts the
# online DataSource with baseUrl '' and Next's rewrite forwards /v1/* to
# SERVER_ORIGIN, so the browser's HTTP base is the page's own origin.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_WS_URL was the wrong SHAPE as well as unread: streamUrl() appends
# /v1/stream itself and rewrites http->ws, so what belongs here is an origin,
# not a socket URL. A documented variable nothing reads is the same trap as a
# proxy rule for a path nothing serves.
#
# SERVER_ORIGIN is read by apps/web/next.config.ts, at dev/build time, to point
# the /v1/* and /api/auth/* rewrites at the API server. Never sent to a browser.
SERVER_ORIGIN=http://localhost:3000
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SERVER_URL is BAKED INTO THE BUNDLE (that is what NEXT_PUBLIC_
# means) and is the ORIGIN the WebSocket is dialled at directly, because Next's
# dev server does not proxy protocol upgrades. Both are in turbo.json's
# globalEnv so a change to either invalidates the cached build rather than
# serving a bundle compiled against the old one.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SERVER_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Test-only
TEST_DATABASE_ADMIN_URL=postgres://gisik:gisik@localhost:54329/postgres

# Playwright, workflow-builder smoke (apps/web/e2e/workflow-builder.spec.ts).
# ALL FOUR ARE OPT-IN AND UNSET BY DEFAULT, and that is deliberate rather than an
# omission: the spec calls test.skip() unless the first three are present, so an
# unconfigured checkout skips it loudly instead of failing. Nothing in CI sets
# them — .github/workflows/ci.yml runs check:env and `turbo run build test
# typecheck` and nothing else, and turbo.json defines no e2e task — so this suite
# is run by hand against a seeded stack. Documented here because check:env reads
# `process.env` accesses wherever they appear, including a spec CI never runs,
# and an undocumented read is an undocumented read.
#
# The spec builds every page.goto from E2E_BASE_URL directly rather than from
# Playwright's shared baseURL, so this is the origin apps/web is actually served
# at, not the API server's.
E2E_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3001
# The workspace the seeded fixtures live in — a ULID, not a name.
E2E_WORKSPACE_ID=
# Playwright storageState files (see `playwright.config.ts`), each holding a
# signed-in session. The ADMIN one is REQUIRED for the suite to run at all: it
# is in the `configured` predicate the skip is built on.
E2E_ADMIN_STORAGE_STATE=
# The MEMBER one is genuinely OPTIONAL — it is absent from that predicate, so the
# suite runs without it and only the non-admin read-only assertions are skipped.
# Stated because the difference is invisible from the variable names.
E2E_MEMBER_STORAGE_STATE=
